  # ============================================================================
  # Kilo Gateway -- anonymous, keyless, free models only (see the note at the top of this file).
  #
  # Read 2026-11-22. The "Authentication" page of the Kilo gateway documentation says "The gateway allows
  # unauthenticated access for free models only" and that anonymous requests "are identified by IP address
  # and are subject to rate limiting (200 requests per hour per IP)"; its "Models and providers" page says the
  # free models' "availability changes" and that "Auto Free may route your requests to providers that log
  # prompts and outputs and use them to improve their services", and of NVIDIA's free endpoints, "Trial use
  # only - do not submit personal or confidential data". The catalogue the gateway serves without a key flags
  # each model below as one that "mayTrainOnYourPrompts". Logging and training are accepted here, as for every
  # free model of this file. The 200 are counted per IP address, so they are one pool for the whole host,
  # shared by every entry of this section and by nothing else here: a slot is a second quota next to
  # OpenRouter's only as long as the pool and each free model last. The "Terms" page, last updated
  # 2026-11-02, was read and not cleared: it allows one account per entity (none is used) and forbids
  # scrapers and circumventing access controls, which a gateway calling the documented API is taken not to
  # be. No entry reads a key, so none belongs to an account: like OmniRoute's, the "api_key" is a literal
  # marker.
  #
  # The marker is not what the gateway sees. LiteLLM's "openai/" provider sends "Authorization: Bearer
  # <api_key>", and the gateway answered 401 "INVALID_TOKEN" to this very marker sent that way (tried by hand
  # the same day), while it serves a call whose header is empty or absent. Each entry therefore blanks the
  # header with "extra_headers", and the proxy then sends an empty one, which the gateway answers 200.
  # "check_compose_template.py" holds every entry on the gateway's base to that, since the 401 would
  # otherwise only show up at run time. "openai/" is cut once by LiteLLM, so the id after it, slash and
  # ":free" included, reaches the gateway whole.
